---
import ModelExplorerLayout from '../../layouts/ModelExplorerLayout.astro';
import BlockSection from '../../components/explorer/BlockSection.astro';
import AttentionHeads from '../../components/explorer/AttentionHeads.astro';
import { GPT2_FLOW } from '../../data/models/gpt2-flow';
import data from '../../data/models/gpt2.json';

const blocks = data.blocks as Record<string, any>;
const flow = GPT2_FLOW;

const percent = (probability: number) => `${(probability * 100).toFixed(1)}%`;
const topCandidate = blocks['gpt2'].outputs[0].candidates[0];
const layerNormIn = blocks['layer-norm'].inputs[0];
const layerNormOut = blocks['layer-norm'].outputs[0];
const finalNormIn = blocks['final-norm'].inputs[0];
const range = (values: number[]) => `${Math.min(...values).toFixed(2)}–${Math.max(...values).toFixed(2)}`;
const generationSteps = blocks['generate'].outputs[0].steps;
---

<ModelExplorerLayout
  title="GPT-2, built from scratch"
  description="GPT-2 124M rebuilt block by block in plain PyTorch, with the real tensors from one prompt at every step."
  flow={flow}
>
  <header class="pt-2">
    <p class="mb-2 text-sm font-semibold text-blue-700">Architectures from scratch · model 1</p>
    <h1 class="mb-4 text-4xl font-bold">GPT-2, built from scratch</h1>
    <p class="mb-4 text-lg text-gray-700">
      GPT-2 is the baseline every later language model is described against. This page rebuilds the
      124M-parameter version top-down: the whole model first, then each box opened up until nothing
      is left but matrix multiplies.
    </p>
    <div class="mb-4 rounded-lg border bg-gray-50 p-4 text-sm text-gray-700">
      <p class="mb-2"><b>How to read it.</b> The chart on the left is the model. Click any block to jump to it;
      as you scroll, the chart shows where you are. Every block has the exact code that ran, and the real
      tensors that went in and came out.</p>
      <p class="mb-2"><b>One prompt, all the way through.</b> Every number below comes from feeding
      <span class="font-mono">“{data.prompt}”</span> to GPT-2 with OpenAI's released weights. It continues
      with <span class="font-mono">“{data.continuation}”</span>. Matrices show all {data.tokens.length} tokens but
      only the first 6 of their columns; blocks inside the transformer are shown for layer {data.traced_layer}.</p>
      <p><b>Checked, not assumed.</b> The code here loads the official weights and matches Hugging Face's GPT-2
      to within {data.max_logit_difference_vs_hf.toExponential(0)} on every logit; greedy generation matches token
      for token.</p>
    </div>
  </header>

  <BlockSection id="gpt2" title="The whole model" flow={flow} block={blocks['gpt2']} level={2}>
    <p>
      From far away GPT-2 is one function: a list of token ids in, a score for every word in its vocabulary
      out. Turn the text into ids, turn each id into a vector, push the vectors through 12 identical
      transformer blocks, then score every possible next token.
    </p>
    <p>
      The shape is the whole story. A sequence of <code>T</code> tokens becomes a <code>[T, 768]</code> matrix
      — one 768-number row per token — and stays that shape through all 12 blocks. Only at the very end does
      each row become 50,257 scores, one per vocabulary entry. For our prompt the top guess is
      <span class="font-mono">“{topCandidate.text}”</span> at {percent(topCandidate.probability)}: the model is
      unsure, and that spread is what it learned.
    </p>
  </BlockSection>

  <BlockSection id="tokenizer" title="Tokenizer" flow={flow} block={blocks['tokenizer']} level={2}>
    <p>
      The network never sees characters. A byte-pair-encoding (BPE) tokenizer cuts text into pieces from a
      fixed vocabulary of 50,257 and replaces each piece with its index. Common words get one id, rare words
      are split into several, and the leading space is part of the token — <code>" Turing"</code> and
      <code>"Turing"</code> are different ids.
    </p>
    <p>
      This is the one block not written from scratch: it is OpenAI's own tokenizer via <code>tiktoken</code>,
      because the weights only make sense with the exact vocabulary they were trained on.
    </p>
  </BlockSection>

  <BlockSection id="embeddings" title="Token + position embedding" flow={flow} block={blocks['embeddings']} level={2}>
    <p>
      Two lookup tables. The token table has one learned 768-number row per vocabulary entry; the position
      table has one per position, 0 to 1023. Each token's vector is its token row plus its position row.
    </p>
    <p>
      The addition is the only place GPT-2 learns where a token is. Without it, attention would see a bag of
      words — “dog bites man” and “man bites dog” would look the same. Later models replace this table with
      rotations inside attention (RoPE, in the Llama page).
    </p>
  </BlockSection>

  <BlockSection id="transformer-block" title="Transformer block" flow={flow} block={blocks['transformer-block']} level={2}>
    <p>
      The block is two steps, each wrapped the same way. <b>Attention</b> lets every token gather information
      from the tokens before it. The <b>MLP</b> then transforms each token on its own. Before each step a
      LayerNorm rescales the input; after each, the step's output is <em>added</em> to what came in.
    </p>
    <p>
      That “normalise, transform, add back” wrapper is called pre-norm residual, and it is why the block can
      be stacked 12 times: the input passes through untouched on the skip path, and each block only writes a
      correction on top. GPT-2 repeats this block 12 times with separate weights, and 85 of its 124 million
      parameters live here.
    </p>
  </BlockSection>

  <BlockSection id="layer-norm" title="LayerNorm" flow={flow} block={blocks['layer-norm']} level={3}>
    <p>
      For each token separately: subtract the mean of its 768 numbers, divide by their standard deviation,
      then apply a learned per-feature <code>scale</code> and <code>shift</code>. The sublayer that follows
      always sees inputs of a predictable size, however large the running sum on the skip path has become.
    </p>
    <p>
      The row statistics show both halves. Going in, each token's standard deviation is
      {range(layerNormIn.row_stds)}. Normalising gives every row mean 0 and standard deviation 1; the learned scale then
      shrinks them to {range(layerNormOut.row_stds)} — this layer was trained to keep its input to attention
      small. The same block appears twice per transformer block and once more at the very end.
    </p>
  </BlockSection>

  <BlockSection id="attention" title="Causal self-attention" flow={flow} block={blocks['attention']} level={3}>
    <p>
      Attention is the only place tokens exchange information. Each token asks a question (its
      <b>query</b>), advertises what it contains (its <b>key</b>), and offers something to pass on (its
      <b>value</b>). A token's output is a weighted average of the values of the tokens it may see, weighted
      by how well its query matches their keys.
    </p>
    <p>
      “Causal” means a token may only look backwards: when predicting what follows <code>" was"</code>, the
      model must not peek at <code>" a"</code>. GPT-2 runs 12 of these attention patterns in parallel
      (heads), each in its own 64-dimensional slice. The seven steps below are the whole of it.
    </p>
  </BlockSection>

  <BlockSection id="qkv-projection" title="Q, K, V projection" flow={flow} block={blocks['qkv-projection']} level={4}>
    <p>
      One linear layer, <code>768 → 2304</code>, then cut into three equal pieces. It is three separate
      projections fused into one matrix multiply: queries, keys and values are each a different learned view
      of the same token.
    </p>
  </BlockSection>

  <BlockSection id="split-heads" title="Split into 12 heads" flow={flow} block={blocks['split-heads']} level={4}>
    <p>
      No arithmetic here, only a reshape. Each token's 768 numbers are read as 12 groups of 64, and the head
      axis moves to the front, so <code>[T, 768]</code> becomes <code>[12, T, 64]</code>. From here on every
      operation runs on all 12 heads at once, and each head attends independently.
    </p>
  </BlockSection>

  <BlockSection id="attention-scores" title="Scores: Q·Kᵀ / √64" flow={flow} block={blocks['attention-scores']} level={4}>
    <p>
      Every query is dotted with every key, giving a <code>[T, T]</code> grid per head: row <i>i</i>, column
      <i>j</i> is how relevant token <i>j</i> looks to token <i>i</i>. Dividing by √64 = 8 keeps the scores
      from growing with the head size, which would push the softmax into giving all its weight to one token.
    </p>
  </BlockSection>

  <BlockSection id="causal-mask" title="Causal mask" flow={flow} block={blocks['causal-mask']} level={4}>
    <p>
      Every score above the diagonal — a token looking at a later token — is set to −∞. After the softmax that
      becomes a weight of exactly zero. This one line is what makes GPT-2 a left-to-right model, and it is
      also why it can be trained on every position of a sequence at once.
    </p>
  </BlockSection>

  <BlockSection id="softmax" title="Softmax" flow={flow} block={blocks['softmax']} level={4}>
    <p>
      Each row of scores becomes a probability distribution: exponentiate, then divide by the row's sum. The
      first token can only see itself, so its row is always <code>[1, 0, 0, 0]</code>.
    </p>
    <p>
      The 12 heads learn very different habits, even in the first layer. Some put almost all their weight on
      the current token; others spread it back over the whole prompt. All 12 are drawn below the tensors.
    </p>
    <AttentionHeads slot="after" weights={data.attention_weights_all_heads} numHeads={data.num_heads}
      tokens={data.tokens} layer={data.traced_layer} />
  </BlockSection>

  <BlockSection id="weighted-sum" title="Weighted sum of V" flow={flow} block={blocks['weighted-sum']} level={4}>
    <p>
      Multiply the weights by the values: each token's output in this head is the mix of earlier tokens'
      values that its weights asked for. This is where information actually moves between positions.
    </p>
  </BlockSection>

  <BlockSection id="merge-heads" title="Merge heads + output projection" flow={flow} block={blocks['merge-heads']} level={4}>
    <p>
      Undo the split — put the 12 heads' 64-number outputs side by side to get back <code>[T, 768]</code> —
      then one more linear layer, <code>768 → 768</code>, which mixes what the heads found and writes the
      result in the residual stream's coordinates.
    </p>
  </BlockSection>

  <BlockSection id="residual" title="Residual add" flow={flow} block={blocks['residual']} level={3}>
    <p>
      The sublayer's output is added to its input rather than replacing it. Two consequences. Gradients have
      a direct path from the loss to every layer, which is what makes a 12-deep stack trainable. And the
      hidden state becomes a shared “residual stream” that every block reads from and writes into.
    </p>
    <p>
      Because blocks only ever add, the stream grows. By the end of layer 11 our tokens' standard deviations
      are {range(finalNormIn.row_stds)}, against {range(layerNormIn.row_stds)} going into layer 0 — which is
      why every sublayer, and the output, needs a LayerNorm in front.
    </p>
  </BlockSection>

  <BlockSection id="mlp" title="MLP" flow={flow} block={blocks['mlp']} level={3}>
    <p>
      The second half of the block works on each token alone: widen to 3072, apply a non-linearity, narrow
      back to 768. Attention moves information between tokens; the MLP is where most of the per-token
      computation — and two thirds of each block's parameters — lives.
    </p>
  </BlockSection>

  <BlockSection id="up-projection" title="Linear 768 → 3072" flow={flow} block={blocks['up-projection']} level={4}>
    <p>
      A plain linear layer, <code>x·Wᵀ + b</code>, into a space four times wider. The extra width gives the
      non-linearity many separate features to switch on and off.
    </p>
  </BlockSection>

  <BlockSection id="gelu" title="GELU" flow={flow} block={blocks['gelu']} level={4}>
    <p>
      GELU is close to ReLU — large positive values pass through, large negative values go to zero — but
      smooth around zero and slightly negative just below it. GPT-2 uses this tanh approximation of
      <code>x·Φ(x)</code>; compare the in and out columns to see negative values squashed toward 0.
    </p>
  </BlockSection>

  <BlockSection id="down-projection" title="Linear 3072 → 768" flow={flow} block={blocks['down-projection']} level={4}>
    <p>
      Back to the residual stream's width, so the result can be added to it.
    </p>
  </BlockSection>

  <BlockSection id="final-norm" title="Final LayerNorm" flow={flow} block={blocks['final-norm']} level={2}>
    <p>
      The same <a href="#layer-norm" class="text-blue-700 hover:underline">LayerNorm</a>, applied once after
      the last block. Going in, the residual stream has grown to standard deviations of
      {range(finalNormIn.row_stds)}; coming out, it is back to a fixed scale for the output layer.
    </p>
  </BlockSection>

  <BlockSection id="lm-head" title="LM head (tied to the embedding)" flow={flow} block={blocks['lm-head']} level={2}>
    <p>
      To score the vocabulary, GPT-2 reuses the token-embedding table: each token's score is the dot product
      of the final hidden vector with that token's embedding. Nothing new is learned here — the input and
      output share one 38.6M-parameter matrix (“weight tying”), which is why this block shows no parameters.
    </p>
    <p>
      The model predicts at every position at once, not only the last. After <code>" was"</code> it expects
      <code>" a"</code>, which is the token that actually came next. During training every position is a
      prediction with a loss; at generation time only the last row is used.
    </p>
  </BlockSection>

  <BlockSection id="generate" title="Pick a token, repeat" flow={flow} block={blocks['generate']} level={2}>
    <p>
      Generation is a loop around the whole model: take the scores after the last token, pick one, append it,
      run again. This page uses the simplest rule, greedy — always the top token — so the output is
      deterministic. Its first pick, <span class="font-mono">“{generationSteps[0].text}”</span>, was only
      {percent(generationSteps[0].probability)} likely; once it is in the context, the next pick,
      <span class="font-mono">“{generationSteps[1].text}”</span>, gets {percent(generationSteps[1].probability)}.
    </p>
    <p>
      Note what this loop does not do: remember anything between steps. Every iteration recomputes all
      tokens from scratch. Real implementations cache each layer's keys and values instead (the KV cache),
      which changes the cost, not the result.
    </p>
  </BlockSection>
</ModelExplorerLayout>
